<Project>

  <!--
    Central Package Management for the Bobcat source tree (src/). One pinned version per
    package so the Bobcat.* projects can never drift apart. The Critter Stack set is the
    canonical, mutually-compatible alignment that consuming samples must also target:

      WolverineFx 6.30.1  ↔  Marten 9.30.0  ↔  JasperFx(.Events) 2.56.0  ↔  Fisher 1.0.4  ↔  Polecat 5.20.0

    Checked against the published nuspecs on 2026-08-28 (issue #172 — the four-source event
    model vehicle needs Wolverine >= 6.30.1 for the event-model command's url flag and chains carrying their
    Event Modeling roles; 6.30.1 rather than 6.30.3 because 6.30.2+ raises the JasperFx floor
    to 2.57.1 and this repo compiles against 2.56.0, which is also CritterWatch's proven pin):
      WolverineFx 6.30.1        requires JasperFx(.Events) >= 2.56.0
      WolverineFx.Marten 6.30.x requires Marten >= 9.23.0
      WolverineFx.Fisher 6.30.x requires Fisher >= 1.0.2
      Marten 9.30.0             requires JasperFx(.Events) >= 2.56.0, Weasel >= 9.27.0
      Fisher 1.0.4              requires JasperFx(.Events) >= 2.56.0, Weasel.Sqlite >= 9.27.0
      Polecat 5.20.0            requires JasperFx(.Events) >= 2.56.0

    One consequence of Marten 9.30.0 + Fisher 1.0.4: both nupkgs bundle the IDENTICAL
    JasperFx.Events.SourceGenerator (byte-for-byte), so a project referencing both stores loads
    the generator twice and every projection's Evolver partial is emitted twice (CS0433/CS0111 —
    jasperfx#462, the same trap CritterWatch documents). Fix, ported from CritterWatch: drop ALL
    store-bundled copies via a DropDuplicateBundledEventSourceGenerator target and compile with
    the explicit JasperFx.Events.SourceGenerator pinned below, so the generator always matches
    the JasperFx.Events runtime whatever the stores happen to bundle.
    Every floor is at or below the pin, so taking the newest of each still lands on ONE
    JasperFx (2.54.0), which is the property that matters — the event types (IEvent, etc.) only
    unify when every package resolves the same JasperFx.Events. Fisher is what forced the
    original bump: every published Fisher (0.5.0+) requires JasperFx.Events >= 2.47.0, and
    nothing on the previous set (WolverineFx 6.24.2 / Marten 9.22.0 / JasperFx 2.37.0) could
    reference it.

    JasperFx(.Events) moved 2.53.0 -> 2.54.0 on 2026-08-21 for the Event Modeling semantic
    model (JasperFx/jasperfx#687, #689), which issues #106/#107/#108 implement against.
    2.53.0 already carried an early sketch of the namespace; 2.54.0 is where #687's RESHAPED
    descriptor landed, and the difference is not cosmetic — the JasperFx.Events.EventModeling
    public surface goes from 65 documented members to 183. What #106 needs specifically arrives
    only in 2.54.0: SpecificationDescriptor (Identity / Feature / Scenario / ResolvedTypes),
    HotspotDescriptor.PendingSpecification + HotspotOrigin (that is #689), SlicePattern,
    TriggerKind, EventModelSliceDescriptor.Domain / Pattern / TriggerKind / Elements / Edges,
    the EventModelElement / EventModelLane / EventModelPalette model, EventModelDiscovery, and
    the AddEventModelSource registration extensions.

    The reshape is BREAKING for anyone already on the 2.53.0 sketch: EventModelSliceBuilder lost
    its single-string constructor and its Command/Emits/HandledBy/Projects/Reads/TriggeredBy
    members, which moved onto ExternallyOwnedRoles and the element/edge model. Nothing in this
    repo referenced JasperFx.Events.EventModeling at the time of the bump, so the break costs
    Bobcat nothing here — but a consuming sample pinned to 2.53.0 would feel it.

    Every other member of the set declares JasperFx(.Events) as a FLOOR (>=), and the highest
    floor in the set is Fisher's and Polecat's 2.53.0, so raising only this one package keeps
    the single-JasperFx property intact. WolverineFx and Marten were deliberately NOT moved
    with it: 6.29.2 and 9.29.0 also land on 2.53.0, they change nothing #106 needs, and the
    aligned set was verified end to end as a unit — moving one package for a named reason is
    not the same as re-taking the set.
    See versions.md for the full matrix and the samples' target set.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Critter Stack — mutually-compatible aligned set -->
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx" Version="6.30.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.RuntimeCompilation" Version="6.30.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.Marten" Version="6.30.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.Fisher" Version="6.30.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Marten" Version="9.30.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="JasperFx" Version="2.56.0" />
    <!--
      Bobcat.CritterStack binds to the JasperFx.Events abstractions (IEventStore, IEventDatabase,
      IProjectionCoordinator, ProjectionScenario) rather than to Marten, so one package serves
      Marten, Polecat and Fisher. Same version as JasperFx — the two ship together.

      Fisher (SQLite, the inner-loop store) and Polecat (SQL Server) are pinned here so that the
      moment a project references them it lands on this JasperFx and no other. Fisher coverage in
      Bobcat.CritterStack.Tests (a temp SQLite file, no docker) and the BankAccountES-on-Fisher
      acceptance are issue #103's follow-on to this bump; Polecat needs SQL Server and is a
      documented manual path (CLAUDE.md, "Bobcat.CritterStack is store-agnostic").
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="JasperFx.Events" Version="2.56.0" />
    <!-- Referenced explicitly (as an analyzer) by any project referencing MORE than one store,
         with the store-bundled copies dropped — see the dual-store note in the header. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="JasperFx.Events.SourceGenerator" Version="2.56.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fisher" Version="1.0.4" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Polecat" Version="5.20.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Alba" Version="8.5.2" />

    <!-- Entity Framework Core (Bobcat.EntityFrameworkCore) -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory" Version="10.0.0" />

    <!--
      Bobcat.Console host. Same 6.29.1 as the aligned set above — the earlier monitor-only
      version split existed only while the rest of the repo was still on Wolverine 6.5.1, and
      collapsed when the alignment caught up.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.Http" Version="6.30.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.SignalR" Version="6.30.1" />
    <!-- MCP server for Bobcat.Console — same package + version CritterWatch's *.Mcp family uses. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore" Version="1.2.0" />
    <!--
      TypeScript contract codegen for the monitor's Vue SPA (CritterWatch's GenerateCommand
      pattern): NJsonSchema reflects the MonitorEvents.cs records through the same STJ settings
      the wire uses, so the .ts mirrors can only ever say what the serializer does.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="NJsonSchema" Version="11.5.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="NJsonSchema.CodeGeneration.TypeScript" Version="11.5.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="YamlDotNet" Version="16.3.0" />

    <!--
      Microsoft.Testing.Platform (Bobcat.Mtp — Bobcat as an MTP test host).

      Pinned to 1.9.1 to match what xunit.v3 3.2.2 builds against (its package is literally named
      xunit.v3.core.mtp-v1). Forcing 2.x here loads a platform assembly whose types have moved,
      and xUnit's auto-registered MSBuild extension dies with a TypeLoadException on
      IDataConsumer. Aligning on the version the xUnit v3 ecosystem uses is also what lets a
      consumer mix Bobcat specs and xUnit v3 tests in one solution — the point of issue #41.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Testing.Platform" Version="1.9.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Testing.Platform.MSBuild" Version="1.9.1" />

    <!-- Roslyn (source generator only) -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" Version="4.12.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Analyzers" Version="3.11.0" />

    <!--
      Test stack. xUnit v3 ships as a Microsoft.Testing.Platform test host, so the test projects
      are self-executing MTP applications rather than VSTest assemblies. That means no
      Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk, no xunit.runner.visualstudio, and no coverlet.collector (a VSTest
      data collector) — MTP supplies its own equivalents.

      This also lets Bobcat's own specs run beside these projects under one runner, which is the
      point of the MTP work in issue #41.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.2.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Shouldly" Version="4.3.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="NSubstitute" Version="5.3.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
